#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Stream Groww market quotes over websockets into the Redis hash `groww:quotes:live`, and keep streaming until
stopped.

    bin/groww/quotes                                 # instruments from the Redis set groww:quotes:subscriptions
    bin/groww/quotes --tokens "NSE|CASH|2885,NSE|FNO|35001"
    bin/groww/quotes --per-socket 500

    redis-cli SADD groww:quotes:subscriptions "NSE|CASH|2885" "BSE|CASH|500325"
    redis-cli HGET groww:quotes:live "NSE:RELIANCE"
    redis-cli HGETALL groww:quotes:instruments

Self-contained: it logs in with `GrowwAPI` and carries its own NATS connection, ed25519 handshake, subscriptions
and protobuf decoding for Groww's feed at `wss://socket-api.groww.in`, so it needs no other feed process or key.

**The transport.** Groww publishes market data on NATS over a websocket. Before connecting, the script generates
a fresh ed25519 key pair and exchanges its public key, in NATS NKEY form, for a short lived socket JWT at
`POST https://api.groww.in/v1/api/apex/v1/socket/token/create/` with the REST access token. The server opens with an
`INFO` frame carrying a nonce; the script answers `CONNECT` with the JWT and the nonce signed by the private key,
then sends one `SUB` per subject. Each instrument has a price subject and a depth subject, for example
`/ld/eq/nse/price_detailed.2885` and `/ld/eq/nse/book.2885`; FNO instruments use `/ld/fo/...`. Payloads are
protobuf `StocksSocketResponseProtoDto` messages. The script carries Groww's file descriptor serialized and builds
the message classes from it at runtime, in a descriptor pool of its own.

**Instruments** are `--tokens` when given, otherwise the members of the Redis set `groww:quotes:subscriptions`,
each as `EXCHANGE|SEGMENT|EXCHANGE_TOKEN` - `NSE` or `BSE`, `CASH` or `FNO`, then Groww's exchange token (a number
for stocks and contracts, the index name such as `NIFTY` for an index). Groww's feed has no subjects for
commodities, so `COMMODITY` tokens are skipped. Each is named from Groww's public instrument file, downloaded at
startup. One websocket carries up to `--per-socket` instruments, default 1000.

**Keys.**

- `groww:quotes:live`, a hash of the latest tick per instrument as JSON, keyed by the instrument's name
  (`NSE:RELIANCE`) - from the instrument file, else from the symbol Groww puts in the payload, else the token.
  Price and depth arrive separately and are merged, so each tick carries the latest of both. It is not cleared at
  startup, so an instrument no longer subscribed keeps its last tick.
- `groww:quotes:instruments`, a hash of every subscribed `EXCHANGE|SEGMENT|EXCHANGE_TOKEN` to its name, or to an
  empty string when the instrument file does not name it. Replaced whole at startup.
- `groww:quotes:stream`, a Redis Stream with every tick in arrival order as a `tick` field, which
  `bin/groww/persist_ticks` writes to `groww.ticks`. Capped at about 100,000 entries, so ticks that arrive while
  the persister is down for long enough are trimmed before they are persisted.

A tick has `id`, `broker`, `instrument_token`, `exchange`, `mode` (`full` once depth has arrived, else `quote`),
`last_price`, `last_quantity`, `average_price`, `volume`, `buy_quantity`, `sell_quantity`, `ohlc`, `change`, `oi`,
`oi_day_high`, `oi_day_low`, `last_trade_time`, `exchange_timestamp`, `depth` (`buy` and `sell` levels of
`quantity`, `price` and `orders`) and `received_at`, with `None` for what Groww does not send - it sends no last
quantity, trade time, open interest range or order counts. Groww sends prices in paise; they are divided into
rupees. Quantities arrive as doubles and are rounded to whole numbers.

**Sessions.** The access token is read afresh on every connect, so a login made by any process is picked up. When
Groww refuses the socket token request (HTTP 401 or 403) or the NATS server reports an authorization error, or a
socket fails to open six times running, it logs in again through `GrowwAPI` - one socket at a time - before
reconnecting. A reconnect otherwise backs off from 1 to 60 seconds.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, 1 when a login fails or a socket still cannot open after logging in
again, and 2 when there is nothing to subscribe to. Reads only - it places no orders.
"""

